  <title>7.2.3: Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01761</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.2.3: Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>25ba18e2-6f38-4962-bc53-e9bc47b76cdb</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Three steps of a growing pattern.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_06_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>Here is a pattern of squares.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Is the number of small squares growing linearly? Explain how you know.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No, there are 3 squares in the first step, 6 squares in the second step, and 11 squares in the third step. So the
      number of additional squares is 3 going from the first to the second step and 5 going from the second to the third
      step.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>Answer questions a–e to complete the table.</item>
      </list>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
        Step
     </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
        Number of small squares
     </emphasis> 
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        3
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        2
      </entry>
            <entry>
        6
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        3
      </entry>
            <entry>
        11
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        4
      </entry>
            <entry>a. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        5
      </entry>
            <entry>b. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        10
      </entry>
            <entry>c. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        12
      </entry>
            <entry>d. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        \(n\)
      </entry>
            <entry>e. _____</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_20799070-1abe-475a-8519-4dec9738d609" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>In Step 4, what is the number of small squares?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Step 4 has 18 small squares.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 18. Step 4 has 18 small squares.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_92cd14c1-8001-44de-a4a8-46e28a78acb7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>In Step 5, what is the number of small squares?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Step 5 has 27 small squares.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 27. Step 5 has 27 small squares.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_613d1789-d831-41a4-b28b-1781ac708a7a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>In Step 10, what is the number of small squares?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Step 10 has 102 small squares.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 102. Step 10 has 102 small squares.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="baa64dc5-ff5e-4fe2-a9af-588bdfb5d003" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para>In Step 12, what is the number of small squares?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Step 12 has 146 small squares.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 146. Step 12 has 146 small squares.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>In Step \(n\), what is the number of small squares?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(n^2+2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Is the number of small squares growing exponentially? Explain how you know.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No, from Step 1 to Step 2, the number of squares doubles. From Step 2 to Step 3, the number of squares increases
      by a factor of \(\frac{11}{6}\). From Step 3 to Step 4, the number of squares increases by a factor of
      \(\frac{18}{11}\). These growth factors are all different; in contrast, for an exponential function, the values
      increase by equal growth factors over equal intervals.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>The nth expression, \(n^2+2\), is an example of a <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic expression</term>. A quadratic expression can be written using a squared
  term. It can be written in other ways as well, but the expression should be able to be written with the highest
  exponent on the variable as 2.</para>
      <list>
        <item>A quadratic relationship is not like a linear relationship because as one quantity increases by a certain amount,
    the second quantity doesn’t increase by the same amount.</item>
        <item>A quadratic relationship is not like an exponential relationship because as one quantity increases by a certain
    amount, the second quantity doesn’t change by the same factor. (In this case, if the number of squares grew
    exponentially, this would mean that, from each step to the next, the number of squares is multiplied by the same
    factor.)</item>
      </list>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            
            
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Han wrote \(n(n+2)-2(n-1)\) for the number of small squares in the design at Step \(n\) in the pattern at the
      beginning of the activity.</para>
          <para>Explain why Han is correct.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>At Step \(n\), there is an \(n\) by \(n+2\) rectangle containing \(n(n+2)\) small squares with two columns of
      \(n-1\) squares removed from either side.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <para>On your own, draw and label what Step 3 would look like when Han was writing his expression.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>I drew a rectangle with three small squares down and five small squares across. Then I removed the two small
      squares from the first column and the last column.</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="Grid with sides labeled.">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_06_02.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
         </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
      <title>Video: Quadratic Expressions</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about quadratic expressions.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9429b37b256ebf82ca64508151eae28b120c65d0"/>
      </media>
    </section>

    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Which of the following is an example of a quadratic expression?</para>
    </note>

    <section>
      <title>Writing Expression from Patterns</title>
      <para>Find the number of dots in Step 5, and write an expression to find \(n\) number of dots.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Make a table to help you find the pattern.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
        Step number
     </emphasis> 
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>
        Number of dots
     </emphasis> 
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
        0
      </entry>
            <entry>
        4
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        1
      </entry>
            <entry>
        7
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        2
      </entry>
            <entry>
        10
      </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
        3
      </entry>
            <entry>
        13
      </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Notice that the start has 2 rows and 2 columns, or 4 dots.
  Each step adds a row of 2 dots, plus 1 more dot.
  So, each step adds 3 dots.</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Continuing this linear pattern, Step 5 would have 19 dots.</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - An expression could be \(3n+4\). 
  This is a linear expression.</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Writing Expression from Patterns</title>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Find the number of dots in Step 5, and write an expression to find \(n\) number of dots.</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_04_08_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to write an expression from a pattern:</para>
            <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
              <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
          Step
       </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                   <emphasis>
          Number of dots
       </emphasis> 
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
          0
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          1
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          1
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          3
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          2
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          9
        </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
          3
        </entry>
                  <entry>
          19
        </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>Each step has that number of dots squared, then doubled, and then one is added.</para>
            <para>So, Step 5 would have 51 dots.</para>
            <para>The expression would be \(2n^2+1\), where \(n\) is the step number.</para>
            <para>This is a quadratic expression.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
